---
import Experience from '../layouts/Experience.astro';
import { orgs } from '../data/profile';
---

<Experience org={orgs.kualia}>
  <p>
    Kualia is a budgeting app that helps people give every dollar a job and feel
    in control of their money. I built it because I was a paying <strong>YNAB</strong>
    user and loved envelope budgeting, but wanted something that fit the way I
    think about my money. Envelope budgeting is far from a new
    idea; I just wanted my own take on it, and I designed, built, and launched it
    entirely on my own.
  </p>

  <p>
    I built the iPhone app with <strong>SwiftUI</strong>, the website with <strong
      >Angular</strong
    >, and the system that powers them both with <strong>Convex</strong>. Being
    the only person on it means I also make the product calls, guided by what
    real users tell me they need.
  </p>

  <p>
    It's live on the App Store today and earns around $300 in recurring revenue
    every month. It's the project I'm proudest of, because every part of
    it, good and bad, is a decision I made and shipped.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot shot--card">
    <img
      src="/images/kualia-main.png"
      alt="The Kualia budget on web and iPhone, showing envelopes for the month with assigned, activity, and available amounts."
      width="3118"
      height="2166"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </figure>

  <h2 class="section">Connecting your budget to AI</h2>

  <p>
    The piece I'm most excited about lately is the <a
      href="https://kualia.com/mcp"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP server<span class="ext" aria-hidden="true"
        >↗</span
      ></a
    >. Kualia speaks the Model Context Protocol, so any assistant
    you already pay for, Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, Perplexity, can read your
    transactions, envelopes, recurring bills, and reports directly, with your
    permission. You ask questions in plain English instead of opening
    dashboards: <em>"What categories am I overspending in this month?"</em> or
    <em>"Which subscriptions haven't I used this quarter?"</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    It does more than read. With your approval it can reassign money between
    envelopes, set assigned amounts, and import a CSV of transactions straight
    from the chat. Read-only tools run on their own; anything that writes asks
    you first.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <img
      src="/images/kualia-mcp.png"
      alt="Claude Code connected to the Kualia MCP server, calling the envelope status tool and reporting overspending across three categories."
      width="2053"
      height="1367"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </figure>

  <p>
    Under the hood it's a JSON-RPC endpoint on the Convex backend, authenticated
    with a full <strong>OAuth 2.1 + PKCE</strong> server I implemented myself, so
    there are no API keys to paste and every token is scoped to your workspace.
    The trick that keeps it sane is a single tool registry: one
    TypeScript array is the source of truth, and both the public MCP server and
    the in-app AI chat run through the same code path and the same scope checks.
    Add a tool once and both surfaces get it.
  </p>

  <p>
    You can see it for yourself at <a
      href="https://kualia.com"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">kualia.com<span class="ext" aria-hidden="true"
        >↗</span
      ></a
    >.
  </p>
</Experience>

<style>
  /* Screenshot figures. `.shot--card` sits the (transparent) app screenshot on
     a soft gradient panel, mirroring the hero treatment on kualia.com; the
     plain `.shot` is for screenshots that already carry their own frame. */
  .shot {
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }
  .shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .shot--card {
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    overflow: hidden;
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 20% 80%, rgba(94, 106, 210, 0.12), transparent 50%),
      radial-gradient(circle at 80% 20%, rgba(229, 229, 229, 0.45), transparent 50%),
      radial-gradient(circle at 40% 40%, rgba(163, 163, 163, 0.18), transparent 60%),
      linear-gradient(135deg, #f6f6fa, #eceaf4);
  }
  .shot--card img {
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
  }
  .section {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1.15rem;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  /* trailing "open in new tab" arrow on prose links; inline-block keeps the
     glyph clear of the link's underline */
  .ext {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 0.12em;
    font-size: 0.78em;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
